DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp  reads  a PNM or PAM image as input, chooses _n_c_o_l_o_r_s colors
       to best represent the image and writes a PNM color map defining them as
       output.   A PAM image may actually contain tuples of any kind, but ppnnmm‐‐
       ccoolloorrmmaapp's concept of the tuple values that  best  represent  the  ones
       present  in the image may not make sense if the tuple type isn't RGB or
       GRAYSCALE.  The design of the program, and the rest of this manual, as‐
       sumes the tuples represent colors.

       You  can  use  this map as input to ppnnmmrreemmaapp on the same input image to
       quantize the colors in that image, I.e. produce a  similar  image  with
       fewer  colors.   ppnnmmqquuaanntt  does both the ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp and ppnnmmrreemmaapp steps
       for you.

       A PNM colormap is a PNM image of any dimensions that contains at  least
       one  pixel  of each color in the set of colors it represents.  The ones
       ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp generates have exactly one  pixel  of  each  color,  except
       where padding is necessary with the --ssqquuaarree option.

       The  quantization  method is Heckbert's "median cut".  See QUANTIZATION
       METHOD ⟨#quant⟩ .

       The output image is of the same format (PBM, PGM, PPM, PAM) as the  in‐
       put  image.   Note that a colormap of a PBM image is not very interest‐
       ing.

       The colormap generally has the same maxval as the input image, but ppnnmm‐‐
       ccoolloorrmmaapp  may  reduce  it if there are too many colors in the input, as
       part of its quantization algorithm.

       ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp works on a multi-image input stream.  In that case, it pro‐
       duces  one colormap that applies to all of the colors in all of the in‐
       put images.  All the images must have the same format, depth, and  max‐
       val  (but  may have different height and width).  This is useful if you
       need to quantize a bunch of images that will form a movie or  otherwise
       be  used  together -- you generally want them all to draw from the same
       palette, whereas computing a colormap separately from each image  would
       make  the  same  color  in  two images map to different colors.  Before
       Netpbm 10.31 (December 2005), ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp ignored any image  after  the
       first.

       If  you want to create a colormap without basing it on the colors in an
       input image, ppaammsseeqq, ppppmmmmaakkee, and ppnnmmccaatt can be useful.


PPAARRAAMMEETTEERRSS
       The single parameter, which is required, is the number  of  colors  you
       want  in the output colormap.  ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp may produce a color map with
       slightly fewer colors than that.  You may specify aallll to get a colormap
       of  every color in the input image (no quantization).  When you specify
       aallll, the function is essentially the same  as  that  of  ppppmmhhiisstt  --mmaapp.
       ppppmmhhiisstt is much older.



